        ":question/id": "96adb906-7cb9-5606-a5fd-4a0f9d2808bf",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-164440",
        ":question/problem": "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,z)=\u27e8x,\\hspace{0.222em}6{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{___}\u27e9\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $y=6{x}^{2}.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$\n\nUse the free parameter directly as its corresponding Cartesian coordinate.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(x,z) & =\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8x,\\hspace{0.222em}6{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}z}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}z}.$\n\nHere the free parameter is $z$. The stated direct-coordinate convention therefore requires the third component to be $z$."
      },
